# Observability

## Purpose

Generate diagnostics that explain API and commerce failures without exposing sensitive information or creating noisy output.

## Scope

Apply to application logging, Spring Boot Actuator, Micrometer metrics available through Actuator, health endpoints, and request correlation.

## Mandatory Rules

- Use SLF4J (`@Slf4j` or `LoggerFactory`) for new logging. Do not add `System.out.println` or `System.err.println`.
- Log at domain boundaries and exceptional outcomes, not every repository call or loop iteration.
- Never log passwords, JWTs, signing secrets, database credentials, authorization headers, or full user/address payloads.
- Include safe identifiers such as order ID, product ID, cart ID, and a correlation ID when available; do not use email or address text as routine identifiers.
- Preserve `/actuator/health` for operational health and `/health` as the current custom welcome endpoint until intentionally consolidated.
- Keep Actuator exposure at `health,info` by default. Any additional endpoint requires a security and operational justification.
- Use Micrometer through Actuator for meaningful counters/timers. Metric tag values must be bounded enums or outcomes, never user IDs, order IDs, emails, names, or URLs with IDs.
- Do not claim distributed tracing or correlation is present. If introduced, add one request filter/interceptor, propagate the ID through MDC, return it safely, and test cleanup between requests.
- Avoid logging the same exception in the service and global handler unless each log adds distinct context.

## Best Practices

- Use parameterized logs: `log.warn("Checkout rejected for productId={} outcome={}", productId, outcome)`.
- Emit business metrics for checkout success/failure, stock conflict, and cancellation only when their names and bounded tags are documented.
- Use timers around external or database-heavy workflows, not trivial getters.
- Make health indicators test connectivity without exposing versions, credentials, or raw exception messages.
- Replace temporary startup printouts with structured logs when editing `StartupSummaryPrinter` or `JwtPropertyVerifier`.

## Anti Patterns

- Printing configuration values during startup.
- Logging complete request/response bodies for users, authentication, checkout, or addresses.
- Metrics tagged by `userId`, `orderId`, `productName`, or exception message.
- Exposing `/actuator/env`, `/actuator/configprops`, or `/actuator/mappings` publicly.
- Catching an exception solely to log and then treating the operation as successful.

## Examples

```java
log.warn("Order cancellation rejected orderId={} status={}",
        order.getOrderId(), order.getStatus());
checkoutCounter.increment();
```

Do not include the JWT, customer email, address, or unbounded exception text in the event or metric tags.

## Checklist

- [ ] Logging uses SLF4J and an appropriate level.
- [ ] Logs contain safe context and no secrets/PII payloads.
- [ ] Metrics are meaningful and low-cardinality.
- [ ] Actuator exposure remains restricted.
- [ ] Health behavior matches documentation.
- [ ] Correlation/tracing is only referenced if actually implemented.
